{"kind":"artifact","text":"cmd/gs at exact clean head 56ed4eeefa3a578d7250733571f34a3e830f5a1f, succeeding 3b38c4e7. Companion to the docs artifact 747c40cf and the internal/nexus artifact at this same head; all three must be co-signed together.\n\nONE TEST, RESTORING COVERAGE THAT WAS LOST WITH A DELETED ONE. Removing the --acknowledge-trusted-processes flag correctly deleted TestServeRequiresExplicitTrustedProcessAcknowledgement rather than inverting it, since its primary subject no longer exists and asserting that serve does not refuse would be noise. But that test also asserted, in passing, that a serve which refuses publishes no resident address. That ordering outlives the flag, and the planner named the loss in its approval without requiring the repair. TestAServeRefusedForANonLoopbackListenAdvertisesNothing asserts it through serveCommand, where the ordering actually lives; TestValidateLoopbackListenRejectsMixedResolution next door exercises the validator in isolation and cannot see it.\n\nMUTATION-CHECKED, AT THIS EXACT HEAD AND AGAIN AFTER THE REBASE. Deleting the validateLoopbackListen guard from serveCommand makes the test fail with \"serve accepted a non-loopback listen address\" — the mutated build printed a real bound address and the posture line, then returned nil. Restoring the guard byte-for-byte returns it to green, with git diff on cmd/gs/main.go empty afterwards. I ran this before the rebase and again after, because a rebase across two of codex's merges is exactly where a carry can quietly reintroduce what it is testing for.\n\nONE LIMIT I AM NAMING RATHER THAN LETTING THE TEST IMPLY MORE THAN IT PROVES. It does not catch the guard merely being reordered to after PublishResident. The deferred withdraw deletes resident.json on the way out, so by the time anything can read ResidentURL the evidence is gone. A version that sampled ResidentURL concurrently while the call was in flight also stayed green, because the publish-then-refuse window is sub-millisecond, so it was measuring nothing and was removed rather than shipped as a loop that looks like it tests ordering and does not. What this test does assert: the refusal happens, it is the loopback refusal specifically and not some other error, and nothing is advertised afterwards. Catching the reorder case needs an observation point that survives the withdrawal, and I did not find a simple honest way to build one.\n\nThe bounded context is the one deviation from the deleted test's context.Background(): a build that serves instead of refusing would otherwise block until the package-wide panic, and failing at a five-second deadline points at the right test.\n\nSECURITY: no authority changes. validateLoopbackListen, the loopback-only resolution, the resident-minted credentials and the posture disclosure are all untouched; this head only asserts an ordering they already had.\n\nREBASE SAFETY: the three-dot and two-dot diffs against fe366843 are identical, so nothing of codex's was reverted. The rebase conflicted only here, in cmd/gs/main_test.go, where codex's connection-limit tests and mine were both inserted at the same point. Resolved by keeping both, codex's first; both sets are present at this head and both pass.\n\nGATES at this exact head: go vet exit 0; go build exit 0; go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, 27 packages ok including internal/docset uncached; gofmt empty; git diff --check clean; worktree clean.","body":{"commit":"56ed4eeefa3a578d7250733571f34a3e830f5a1f","path":"cmd/gs"}}